---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import FinancialStatementKey from "../../components/FinancialStatementKey.astro";
import Glossary from "../../components/Glossary.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import HistoryTimeline from "../../components/HistoryTimeline.astro";
import history from "../../data/history.json";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/ifric/ifric14.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.summary}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope & Definitions" },
        { id: "s3", label: "The Asset Ceiling Recap" },
        { id: "s4", label: "Economic Benefits Available as a Refund" },
        { id: "s5", label: "Economic Benefits Available as a Reduction in Contributions" },
        { id: "s6", label: "Minimum Funding Requirements Explained" },
        { id: "s7", label: "When an MFR Creates an Additional Liability" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Worked Example — MFR Triggering a Liability" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Disclosure" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Judgments & Estimates" },
        { id: "s11", label: "IFRIC 14 vs US GAAP" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Related Standards" },
        { id: "s14", label: "Glossary" },
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>

  <div class="wrap">

    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Why IFRIC 14 exists as a bridge interpretation</div>
        <div class="principle">IAS 19 introduced an "asset ceiling" rule — the present value of any economic benefits available through a defined benefit plan surplus cannot exceed the present value of those economic benefits. IFRIC 14 clarifies what "available" means, specifically addressing how a minimum funding requirement (MFR) imposed by a national regulator affects whether a surplus's economic benefits are genuinely available to the entity.</div>
        <div class="note">Without IFRIC 14, the asset ceiling would be calculated inconsistently across jurisdictions — entities in countries with statutory MFRs could end up with materially different balance sheets than those without, despite identical plan economics.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cs">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Two potential sources of economic benefit from a surplus</div>
        <div class="principle">A defined benefit plan surplus can generate economic benefits in TWO ways: (1) a REFUND of surplus assets by the plan, or (2) a REDUCTION in FUTURE CONTRIBUTIONS. IFRIC 14 examines each pathway separately, because the MFR may block one pathway while leaving the other genuinely available.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope & Definitions</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">What IFRIC 14 covers</div>
        <div class="principle">IFRIC 14 applies to ALL defined benefit plans where the entity's right to a refund or a reduction in future contributions is SUBJECT to a minimum funding requirement (MFR) imposed by a national statute, regulator, or similar authority. It applies whether the surplus is measured under the projected unit credit method or any other actuarial method.</div>
        <div class="elabel">TYPICAL FACT PATTERNS</div>
        <div class="dot-list">
          <span>Pension plans in jurisdictions with statutory MFRs (e.g. UK, US, EU member states)</span>
          <span>Post-employment medical benefit plans subject to funding rules</span>
          <span>Multi-employer plans where individual employers face an MFR</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Key definitions</div>
        <div class="glossary-item">
          <div class="glossary-term">Asset ceiling</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">The present value of any economic benefits available through a defined benefit plan surplus, as calculated under IAS 19.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;">
          <div class="glossary-term">Minimum funding requirement (MFR)</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">A requirement, imposed by a national statute, regulator, or similar authority, that an employer contribute or maintain a minimum level of assets in a defined benefit plan to fund benefits.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>The Asset Ceiling Recap</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="ctitle cn">IAS 19's fundamental constraint</div>
        <div class="principle">IAS 19 restricts the carrying amount of a net defined benefit ASSET to the PRESENT VALUE OF ECONOMIC BENEFITS AVAILABLE — the so-called "asset ceiling." If the actuarial present value of the plan surplus exceeds the present value of economic benefits available through refunds or reduced contributions, the net asset is written down to the ceiling. IFRIC 14 clarifies HOW to determine those economic benefits when an MFR exists.</div>
        <div class="formula-box">
          <div class="f-label">ASSET CEILING FORMULA</div>
          <div class="f-eq">Net defined benefit asset recognised = MINIMUM of (Actuarial present value of surplus, Present value of economic benefits available)</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Economic Benefits Available as a Refund</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">The refund pathway</div>
        <div class="principle">A refund is available when the plan permits the entity to receive a CASH DISTRIBUTION of the surplus. The present value of this economic benefit is the PV of the refund, CALCULATED USING THE SAME DISCOUNT RATE as the defined benefit obligation.</div>
        <div class="elabel">THE MFR BLOCKS A REFUND ONLY IF</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span>The MFR requires the entity to MAINTAIN a minimum funding level that exceeds the surplus</span>
          <span>This means a refund would directly REDUCE the plan assets below the MFR</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">When the MFR does NOT block the refund</div>
        <div class="principle">If the MFR can be satisfied WITHOUT reducing plan assets below the required level — for example, because the surplus exceeds the MFR by enough to allow a partial refund — then the portion of the surplus that can be refunded IS an economic benefit available, and the asset ceiling is calculated accordingly.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Economic Benefits Available as a Reduction in Contributions</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs">
        <div class="ctitle cs">The contribution-reduction pathway</div>
        <div class="principle">If a refund is NOT available because the MFR blocks it, the entity must assess whether a REDUCTION IN FUTURE CONTRIBUTIONS is available. This pathway exists where the entity's required contributions in future periods are LESS than the contributions that would otherwise be required for the plan's ongoing operations.</div>
        <div class="elabel">KEY RULE</div>
        <div class="dot-list">
          <span>If BOTH pathways are blocked by the MFR, the net defined benefit asset is ZERO — the asset ceiling constraint reduces the carrying amount to nil</span>
          <span>If at least one pathway is genuinely available, the asset ceiling is the PV of the available benefits through that pathway</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Interaction with the plan's future service period</div>
        <div class="principle">The entity's right to reduce future contributions extends ONLY over the periods in which it would otherwise have been required to make contributions. If the entity's obligation to contribute extends indefinitely, the PV of the reduction is calculated over a longer horizon — materially affecting the asset ceiling.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Minimum Funding Requirements Explained</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2">
        <div class="ctitle cw">The full picture of how MFRs interact with surplus availability</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-13-1"><span>Aspect</span><span>IFRIC 14 guidance</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-13-1"><span>Definition of MFR</span><span>Any requirement imposed by statute, regulator, or similar authority to contribute or maintain minimum plan assets — including through a plan's own rules that operate like a statutory requirement</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-13-1"><span>What the MFR restricts</span><span>The entity's ability to receive a REFUND or reduce future contributions — it does NOT affect the amount of the net defined benefit asset under IAS 19 before applying the asset ceiling</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-13-1"><span>Calculating availability</span><span>The entity must calculate the present value of the MAXIMUM refund or contribution reduction permitted WITHOUT violating the MFR</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout warn callout--flush">⚠️ An MFR that requires the entity to MAINTAIN a minimum asset level in the plan DOES restrict the economic benefits available — even if the plan's own assets exceed the MFR, the entity may not be able to access the surplus without breaching the requirement.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>When an MFR Creates an Additional Liability</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn">
        <div class="ctitle cn">The additional liability concept</div>
        <div class="principle">IFRIC 14 goes further than just capping the net asset at zero. In some circumstances, the MFR may create an ADDITIONAL LIABILITY on the balance sheet — beyond the defined benefit obligation itself. This arises where the entity is REQUIRED to make EXTRA contributions to restore the plan to the MFR level, and those contributions are not available for refund or as a reduction in future contributions.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">When the additional liability arises</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-13-1"><span>Condition</span><span>Outcome</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-13-1"><span>Plan assets below MFR, entity must fund the deficit to restore MFR</span><span>Additional liability equals the present value of the required contributions</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-13-1"><span>Plan assets above MFR, but MFR still restricts refunds</span><span>No additional liability — only the asset ceiling may limit the net asset</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-13-1"><span>MFR expires before the plan obligation</span><span>After expiry, the additional liability is adjusted — the entity regains access to plan assets</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout info">The additional liability is measured at the PRESENT VALUE of the required contributions, discounted using the SAME DISCOUNT RATE as the defined benefit obligation.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Worked Example — MFR Triggering a Liability</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="worked">
          <div class="wk-title">Example: MFR blocking refund access</div>
          <div class="wk-body">A UK pension plan has a present value of obligation of £50m, plan assets of £65m, giving a surplus of £15m. The plan's MFR requires minimum assets of £60m. The entity expects to contribute £3m/year for the next 3 years, then the MFR expires. The IAS 19 discount rate is 4.5%.</div>
          <div class="wk-answer"><strong>Step 1 — Refund availability:</strong> £65m assets - £60m MFR = £5m maximum refund, but MFR prevents any refund while it applies.<br><strong>Step 2 — Contribution reduction:</strong> With MFR applying, the entity cannot reduce contributions — they must maintain the £60m floor.<br><strong>Step 3 — Additional liability:</strong> Required contributions = £3m/year × 3 years, discounted at 4.5% = £8.3m. Recognised as an additional liability.<br><strong>Step 4 — Asset ceiling:</strong> Since both refund and contribution reduction are blocked by the MFR, the net defined benefit asset is capped at ZERO.<br><strong>Balance sheet impact:</strong> DBO £50m (liability), Additional liability £8.3m (liability), Net asset £0.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Disclosure</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">What IFRIC 14 requires</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span>Description of the minimum funding requirement and how it restricts the availability of economic benefits from the plan surplus</span>
          <span>The present value of any additional liability recognised under IFRIC 14</span>
          <span>When the additional liability is expected to reverse (e.g. expiry of MFR)</span>
          <span>The effect of the asset ceiling on the net defined benefit asset (or asset)</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Judgments & Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>Whether the MFR genuinely restricts access to surplus (e.g. whether a partial refund is permissible)</span>
              <span>Whether plan rules that operate like an MFR are within the scope of IFRIC 14</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div>
            <div class="dot-list">
              <span>Timing and amount of future contributions required to restore the plan to the MFR level</span>
              <span>When the MFR restriction will cease to apply (expiry date)</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>IFRIC 14 vs US GAAP</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — IFRIC 14 vs ASC 715 (Compensation — Retirement Benefits)</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-12-1-1"><span>Feature</span><span>IFRIC 14 (IFRS)</span><span>US GAAP (ASC 715)</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-12-1-1"><span>Asset ceiling</span><span>Net asset limited to PV of economic benefits available</span><span>Similar asset limitation concept applies</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-12-1-1"><span>MFR treatment</span><span>If MFR blocks both refund and contribution reduction, net asset capped at zero; additional liability may arise</span><span>MFR restrictions considered in asset ceiling calculation; no explicit additional liability guidance</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-12-1-1"><span>Additional liability</span><span>PV of required contributions to restore MFR, if MFR blocks surplus access</span><span>Not explicitly addressed — practice may differ</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-12-1-1"><span>Disclosure</span><span>Specific IFRIC 14 disclosure requirements</span><span>ASC 715 disclosure requirements, but no MFR-specific guidance</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Where every IFRIC 14 event lands</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-16-1-14"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Asset ceiling reducing net asset to zero</span><span><span class="badge bp">SOPL</span> <span class="badge bs">SOFP</span></span><span>Recognised in other comprehensive income under IAS 19</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-16-1-14"><span>Additional liability recognised</span><span><span class="badge bs">SOFP</span></span><span>Separate liability beyond the defined benefit obligation</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Additional liability remeasured</span><span><span class="badge bp">SOPL</span> <span class="badge bs">SOFP</span></span><span>Changes in actuarial assumptions affect the PV of required contributions</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-16-1-14"><span>MFR expires, additional liability reverses</span><span><span class="badge bp">SOPL</span> <span class="badge bs">SOFP</span></span><span>Net asset ceiling now reflects the full surplus, not restricted by MFR</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Related Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th cols-08-2"><span>Standard</span><span>Connection</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>IAS 19</span><span>The parent standard supplying the asset ceiling rule and overall defined benefit accounting model</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-08-2"><span>IAS 37</span><span>General provision recognition principles inform the assessment of whether an MFR obligation is a present obligation</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>IFRIC 21</span><span>Shares the underlying theme of identifying the precise obligating event for statutory payment obligations</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cn">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Quick-reference glossary</div>
        <div class="glossary-item">
          <div class="glossary-term">Asset ceiling</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">The present value of any economic benefits available through a defined benefit plan surplus, as calculated under IAS 19 and clarified by IFRIC 14.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;">
          <div class="glossary-term">Minimum funding requirement</div>
          <div class="glossary-def">A statutory or regulatory requirement that an employer maintain a minimum level of assets in a defined benefit plan, potentially restricting access to surplus.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <Glossary entries={[
      { term: "Asset ceiling", definition: "The present value of any economic benefits available through a defined benefit plan surplus — the maximum amount that can be recognised as a net defined benefit asset under IAS 19, as clarified by IFRIC 14." },
      { term: "Minimum funding requirement (MFR)", definition: "A requirement, imposed by a national statute, regulator, or similar authority, that an employer contribute or maintain a minimum level of assets in a defined benefit plan." },
      { term: "Additional liability", definition: "A liability beyond the defined benefit obligation itself, arising under IFRIC 14 when an MFR requires the entity to make extra contributions to restore plan assets to the MFR level." },
      { term: "Refund pathway", definition: "One of the two pathways through which a defined benefit plan surplus can generate economic benefits — receiving a cash distribution of surplus assets from the plan." },
      { term: "Contribution-reduction pathway", definition: "The alternative pathway through which a surplus provides economic benefits — by reducing the entity's future required contributions to the plan." }
    ]} />

    <FinancialStatementKey />

  </div>
  <section id="history">
    <HistoryTimeline items={history.IFRIC14} />
  </section>
</StandardPageLayout>
